The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (likely(nr < NR_syscalls))
    regs->gprs[2] = sys_call_table[nr](regs);
```
Reason:
The syscall number `nr` is controlled by userspace. Although the bounds check `nr < NR_syscalls` is present, it is not followed by a speculation barrier. During speculative execution, the CPU may execute the array index `nr` even when it is out of bounds, leading to a speculative out-of-bounds read from `sys_call_table`. This can be exploited by an attacker to infer kernel memory contents via a cache timing side-channel (Spectre v1).

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (likely(nr < NR_syscalls)) {
    nr = array_index_nospec(nr, NR_syscalls);
    regs->gprs[2] = sys_call_table[nr](regs);
}
```
Reason:
The `array_index_nospec()` macro inserts a speculation barrier (e.g., a data dependency or an `lfence` on x86; on s390 it uses a serialization instruction) that prevents the CPU from speculatively executing the memory access with an out-of-bounds index. By sanitizing `nr` to fall within the valid range even under speculative execution, the fix eliminates the side-channel vulnerability. The bounds check is still performed, but the speculative execution cannot bypass it to access unintended entries.

